            <ArticleTitle>A parent-child relationship scale derived from the child and adolescent psychiatric assessment (CAPA).</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>945-53</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To examine a measure of children's perception of their relationships with parents.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">The Child and Adolescent Psychiatric Assessment (CAPA) was administered to 114 inpatients (aged 9-18 years) at a tertiary asthma center from 1991 to 1994. Ten items from the CAPA were developed as a separate scale, the Parent-Child Relationship Scale (PCRS). Some subjects (82) were given family and child assessments.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The PCRS had good internal reliability (Cronbach alpha = .72). Construct validity was demonstrated by correlations with child and parent report on the Family Assessment Device (r= 0.46 and 0.35; p &lt; .001) and high expressed emotion of the parent (t= 2.89; p &lt; .01). Divergent validity may be evidenced by the fact that the PCRS was not significantly related to high emotional over-involvement. Predictive validity was shown by significant correlations with the total problem scores of parents' (r = 0.28; p &lt; .01) and children's (r = 0.41; p &lt; .001) Achenbach reports, and prediction of CAPA psychiatric diagnosis (OR = 5.83; 95% CI 1.80-22.63).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The PCRS can potentially be used to assess the child's perspective of the parent-child relationship for research or clinical purposes and deserves further study.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
